# The package ships a complete Java web application: the WAR that Maven built
# from this source tree, exploded. Everything flagged below is content of that
# artefact, not a packaging decision, and none of it can be replaced with a
# distribution-packaged copy without rebuilding CARLOS against versions it was
# never tested with. The full component inventory is published as a CycloneDX
# SBOM with each CARLOS release.

# Bundled fonts. The eForm renderer and the print stylesheets reference these by
# path inside the webapp; repointing them at /usr/share/fonts would change how
# printed clinical documents lay out, which is a validation question, not a
# packaging one.
duplicate-font-file
truetype-font-prohibits-installable-embedding

# Third-party jars that ship .java alongside .class, and jars carrying
# multi-release entries for a JDK newer than the one this runs on (the JVM
# simply ignores the versions/ directories above its own).
jar-contains-source
unknown-java-class-version

# Bundled JavaScript, and jars whose payload is resources or a service
# manifest rather than classes. Same reasoning: it is the built artefact.
embedded-javascript-library
codeless-jar

# Legacy files inside the upstream application tree: a thumbnail cache
# directory committed years ago, and a Brazilian-Portuguese copy of the GPL in
# a non-UTF-8 encoding. Both are inert; removing them from the WAR is an
# upstream change, not something to patch in at package time.
package-contains-xvpics-dir
national-encoding

# carlosctl is the deprecated compatibility name for carlos-ctl (renamed to
# match the carlos-podman tool); both resolve to the same binary and the man
# page is carlos-ctl(8).
no-manual-page usr/sbin/carlosctl

# This guarded call is only `systemctl reset-failed`, for clearing the
# application unit's package-driven restart counter. It does not start, stop,
# enable or disable a service; those lifecycle operations use
# deb-systemd-invoke so policy-rc.d remains authoritative.
maintainer-script-calls-systemctl [postinst:221]

# Ubuntu 26.04's man-db exits 3 with this status-only pipeline message for
# compressed manual pages under lintian's `man -l -Tutf8 -Z` invocation; the
# same command also fails for /usr/share/man/man1/ls.1.gz. The uncompressed
# package test gate separately checks the uncompressed source with groff -ww
# and explicitly fails on any diagnostic (groff warnings normally exit zero),
# so real roff problems remain release failures.
groff-message command exited with status 1: * [usr/share/man/man8/carlos-ctl.8.gz:1]

# The eForm renderer (folded in from the former carlos-emr-eform-renderer
# package): an upstream Chromium build, unpacked under
# /usr/lib/carlos-emr/chromium. Both tags below are properties of that binary,
# not packaging decisions, and neither can be resolved without building Chromium
# from source -- which is not a thing this project can carry. Ubuntu 26.04 offers
# no Chromium .deb to depend on instead (the archive's chromium-* packages are
# transitional shims for the snap, and a confined snap cannot read the
# renderer's working directories under /var/lib/carlos-emr). See debian/copyright
# for the licensing, and debian/chromium.pin for the exact revision and checksums.
#
# Chromium statically links its own copies of several libraries. That is
# upstream's build configuration; the distribution copies are not
# interchangeable with the versions Chromium was tested and shipped against.
embedded-library
# Deliberately not stripped. debian/rules skips dh_strip because the binary is
# vendored rather than built here: stripping a prebuilt Chromium discards the
# symbols its own crash handler needs, and would make any renderer crash report
# unusable. The size cost is accepted for that reason.
unstripped-binary-or-object
